<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Chapter 2</a> &gt; Level 19</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 19 Walkthrough: Bookstore</h1>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt frame by frame from two independent recordings of the current build — a 4:58 playthrough extracted at one frame per second and read at full resolution, plus a 3:33 second run used as a cross-check. Both locks were read twice and both agree: the colour keypad settles on <b>01 · 03 · 06 · 04</b> and the EXIT keypad on <b>5831</b> in each run, and the four letter plates read E=5, X=8, I=3, T=1 in both. Where the capture cannot settle a detail — the tile layout of the cover-matching board, the exact pickup that produces the X=8 note, and the meaning of the A6467 and 430 wall pieces — this page says so under "What this page could not verify" instead of guessing. On-screen text was read with optical character recognition on the raw frames and then checked by eye, and every timestamp points at the second in the embedded video.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={2} current={19} variant="jump" label="Chapter 2 · No Honor Among Thieves" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>Two locks, two codes. The door to the back room takes the four poster counts as colour-plus-number pairs — <b>pink 1, yellow 3, red 6, purple 4</b>, display <b>01 · 03 · 06 · 04</b>. The exit door takes <b>5831</b>, which is the word <b>EXIT</b> spelled with the four plates E=5, X=8, I=3, T=1. Finish worth <b>+50 coins</b>.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Read the framed poster above the desk: the note beside it lists <b>Pink: 1, Red: 6, Purple: 4, Yellow: 3</b>.</li>
        <li>Clear the <b>book-cover matching board</b> and pick up the <b>green Book</b> whose cover carries a key symbol.</li>
        <li>Take the <b>Folded paper</b> from the bag, <b>Examine</b> it → <b>E=5</b>.</li>
        <li>On the wooden door's keypad press <b>a colour, then its count</b>: pink 01, yellow 03, red 06, purple 04 → the door opens.</li>
        <li>In the back room: the desk drawer gives <b>X=8</b>, the <b>Metal plate</b> gives <b>I=3</b>.</li>
        <li>Use the <b>Knife</b> on the <b>Teddy bear</b> → <b>Key part</b> → <b>Key</b>; the Key opens the chest on the red rug → <b>T=1</b>.</li>
        <li>Type <b>5831</b> into the keypad beside the blue <b>EXIT</b> sign → results panel, <b>+50 coins</b>.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
            <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="fM0EQM6E7ZY" data-title="Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 19 Bookstore walkthrough"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 19 Bookstore walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/fM0EQM6E7ZY/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 19 Bookstore walkthrough" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/fM0EQM6E7ZY/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l19-hero-bookstore.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 2 Level 19 Bookstore: a reddish-brown bookcase stacked with books in the centre, a striped rug and a ladder against the back shelves, and a pale counter with an antique till and a red SALE bag on the right" width="1200" height="594" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The Bookstore is the nineteenth room of Chapter 2, and it is the first level in this chapter that gives you two rooms to work in rather than one. The shop floor is a proper little bookshop: a big reddish-brown bookcase packed with hardbacks stands in the middle of the floor with more stacks piled around its base, a green-and-red striped runner lies over the dark wooden boards, a rolling ladder leans against the built-in shelves that run across the back wall, and a pale counter on the right carries an antique crank till, a stack of notebooks and a red <b>SALE</b> bag leaning against its front. A desk with a candle and a hutch of framed pictures sits in the back corner, a red <b>FOR SALE</b> placard is clipped to the shelf beside the EXIT door, and the wall above the desk carries the green motivational poster that half this level hangs on.</p>
    <p>The second room is behind the wooden door next to the tall narrow cabinet, and it is a different kind of space entirely: a living room with an olive-green sofa, a red rug, a coffee table, a bookcase with glass doors, a window with mustard curtains and a wall clock. It is not an exit and it is not a bonus area — it is where three of the four letter plates live, which means the level's real answer is split across both rooms. Players who never open that door will finish the shop floor and stand at the EXIT keypad with one digit in their head.</p>
    <p>Two structural facts explain almost every way this level goes wrong. First, the exit door is in the shop, not in the back room, and it is a keypad rather than a lock — so the chest, the key and the teddy bear are not the way out, they are the way to the last digit. Second, the door between the two rooms is a colour lock, not a key lock, and it wants a colour <em>and</em> a number for each of its four slots. Both of those things are unusual for this game, and both of them are why the Bookstore gets searched for a key that was never needed and typed at with digits that were never enough.</p>

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Every object in the Bookstore that takes an input or gives you something, in the order you are likely to meet it.</p>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Where</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Motivational poster</td><td>Wall above the desk, back corner of the shop</td><td>Mint-green print, <b>EVERY great DREAM BEGINS with a DREAMER</b>, with bunting flags in four colours. The note beside it gives the counts <b>1 / 6 / 4 / 3</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Book-cover board</td><td>Shop floor, centre</td><td>The level's matching puzzle, titled <b>Matching The Same Symbols</b>: ornate covers with embossed symbols.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Green Book</td><td>Bookcase, left wall</td><td>An inventory item stamped with a <b>key symbol</b> on the cover. A pointer, not a tool.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Desk drawer</td><td>Desk in the back corner</td><td>Holds a green book and a parchment marked with a large <b>X</b> — the <b>X=8</b> note.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Wooden door keypad</td><td>Wooden door, left of the desk</td><td>Four colour buttons plus a 1-9 pad. Opens the back room. Display <b>01 · 03 · 06 · 04</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Framed wall</td><td>Back room, above the sofa</td><td>Posters <b>Perfume</b> and <b>WILDE</b>, a California plate <b>A6467</b>, a plaque <b>430</b> and a single letter <b>I</b>. Decor, as far as either recording shows.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Teddy bear</td><td>Back room</td><td>Cut it with the <b>Knife</b> and the item relabels itself <b>Key part</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Padlocked chest</td><td>Back room, on the red rug in front of the sofa</td><td>Heart-shaped padlock. The <b>Key</b> opens it; the <b>T=1</b> note is inside.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>EXIT door keypad</td><td>Shop, right wall, under the blue EXIT sign</td><td>Plain four-digit pad. <b>5831</b>.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:04</span>
      <strong>Read the shop floor before you touch anything</strong>
      The opening sweep is the only clean wide shot you get; after this the camera starts zooming and the UI panels start covering things. What matters on the first pass: the poster above the desk in the back corner, the wooden door and its keypad immediately left of that desk, the tall narrow cabinet beside the door that looks like a second door and is not, the book-cover board in the middle of the floor, and the EXIT door on the right wall with the blue sign over it. Everything else — the till, the SALE bag, the stacks on the floor, the cat — is scenery. Note where the two doors are, because that is the whole geography of the level: one door goes to the back room, one door leaves.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fM0EQM6E7ZY&t=4s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:04</a></div>
    </div>
    <InContentAd />

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:32</span>
      <strong>The green Book with the key on its cover</strong>
      By the half-minute mark the bag already holds one item, and it is a small green volume labelled simply <b>Book</b>. Examine it and the cover carries an embossed <b>key symbol</b> — no title, no number, just a key. It is the level's first piece of signposting and it is deliberately misleading: the picture says "find a key", and there is a key in this level, but the key opens a chest in the back room and has nothing to do with the exit. If you spend the next ten minutes hunting a keyed door on the shop floor, this book is why.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fM0EQM6E7ZY&t=32s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:32</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:52</span>
      <strong>The poster, the bunting and the four colour counts</strong>
      Tap the framed print on the wall above the desk and the camera pushes in on a mint-green motivational poster: <b>EVERY great DREAM BEGINS with a DREAMER</b>, with an orange crescent, a dark hand reaching up, and two strings of triangular bunting flags across the top in pink, red, purple and yellow. The flags are the puzzle. Beside the poster, four lines are printed in white: <b>Pink: 1</b>, <b>Red: 6</b>, <b>Purple: 4</b>, <b>Yellow: 3</b>. Those four numbers belong to the keypad on the door across the room, and the order they are listed in is <em>not</em> the order the keypad's buttons sit in — which is the first trap of the level.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l19-poster-colour-counts.jpg" alt="The motivational poster in the Bookstore with the note beside it reading Pink 1, Red 6, Purple 4, Yellow 3" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fM0EQM6E7ZY&t=52s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:52</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 1:00</span>
      <strong>"Matching The Same Symbols" — the book-cover board</strong>
      The board on the shop floor is the level's only conventional puzzle and the game labels it for you: <b>Matching The Same Symbols</b>. The white caption sits on the left of the screen and the board is the pile of ornate covers on the right: each one is embossed with a single emblem — a puzzle piece, a crescent, a treble clef, a butterfly, a triskelion, a starburst, an ornate diamond — and you pair covers that carry the same emblem. The covers are stacked and overlapping rather than laid out in a clean grid, so part of the puzzle is simply seeing what is underneath. Work it by symbol, not by cover colour: the covers repeat their colours across different symbols, so a red cover with a butterfly and a red cover with a puzzle piece are not a pair, and treating colour as the match is what turns a two-minute board into a ten-minute one.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l19-book-cover-puzzle.jpg" alt="The book-cover matching board in the Bookstore with the white caption Matching The Same Symbols on the left, and ornate covers carrying a treble clef, a butterfly, a triskelion and a starburst" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fM0EQM6E7ZY&t=60s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:00</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 1:39</span>
      <strong>The Folded paper opens into E=5</strong>
      Open the bag and there is a <b>Folded paper</b> in it. Select it, press <b>Examine</b>, and the fold opens into a torn sheet with <b>E=5</b> written on it in a serif hand; the item renames itself <b>Piece of paper</b> and stays in the bag as a permanent clue. This is the first of four letter plates and the most important thing on the page to understand: the Bookstore is not collecting numbers, it is collecting letters. E, X, I, T, in that order, are the exit. A player who reads E=5 as "the letter E is worth five points" and moves on has the right idea and the wrong scale.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l19-note-e5.jpg" alt="The Piece of paper item examined in the Bookstore inventory, showing E=5 written on a torn sheet" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=fM0EQM6E7ZY&t=99s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:39</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 1:57</span>
      <strong>The colour keypad: a colour first, then its number</strong>
