    <h2>The mechanism, taken apart<span class="accent">.</span></h2>

    <h3>Two locks that teach two different habits</h3>
    <p>Most rooms in Rooms &amp; Exits hand you one code with one input. The Bookstore hands you two locks that look alike — both are dark panels with a display, both sit on doors — and then asks you to use your hands two completely different ways. The first lock is a <b>paired entry</b>: a colour, then a count, four times, in the order the colour buttons are stacked. The second is a <b>straight substitution</b>: a letter-value table that turns a word into four digits, typed in one run. If you carry the habit from the first lock into the second, you will look for something to colour-code on the EXIT door and find nothing; if you carry the habit from the second into the first, you will try to type 1-6-4-3 as a plain number and the display will sit there and do nothing.</p>

    <h3>Why the exit spells a word instead of a number</h3>
    <p>The four plates are not a set of digits with letters attached — they are a set of letters with digits attached, and the letters are the message. Look at what the level actually gives you: <b>E=5</b>, <b>X=8</b>, <b>I=3</b>, <b>T=1</b>. Four letters that only make sense in one order, and that order is printed above the door you are trying to open. The digits then fall out mechanically: 5, 8, 3, 1. This is why every plate matters and no plate is optional — three of them give you 5-8-3 and a hole where the last digit goes, and the hole is exactly where the level puts the chest, the key, the bear and the knife. The chest is not a reward, it is the fourth digit.</p>
    <p>Notice also how the values are chosen. E=5, X=8, I=3, T=1 gives 5831, a code with no repeats and no zero, which is a small mercy: every digit is a different keypress on a pad where 0 sits at the bottom, and no two plates can be confused for each other by a player reading a photograph of their inventory at an angle. Compare that with the colour lock, where two of the four numbers repeat a colour family and the display's dots are the only thing separating a pink 1 from a red 6 at a glance.</p>

    <h3>The poster is a counting puzzle wearing a quote</h3>
    <p>The mint-green print is doing double duty. As decor it is a motivational poster — <b>EVERY great DREAM BEGINS with a DREAMER</b>, a crescent moon, a silhouetted hand reaching up. As a puzzle it is a tally sheet: two strings of triangular bunting run across the top of the print in pink, red, purple and yellow, and the counts of each colour are the four numbers the back-room lock wants. The note beside the poster prints the answer as four lines — <b>Pink: 1, Red: 6, Purple: 4, Yellow: 3</b> — so you never have to count under pressure, but the counting is the intended solve and it explains why the numbers are so oddly distributed: one pink flag, six red, four purple, three yellow, which is what a real string of bunting looks like when someone hangs it by eye.</p>
    <p>The sting is the ordering. The note lists the colours pink, red, purple, yellow. The keypad's buttons are stacked pink, yellow, red, purple. The display fills from left to right in <em>button</em> order, which is why the accepted state reads 01 · 03 · 06 · 04 and not 01 · 06 · 04 · 03. Two runs, two different players, same result — this is not a coin flip, it is a lock that maps each colour slot independently and shows you the four dots so you can see which slot is which.</p>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>Typing digits at the colour lock.</b> The panel is 80% a normal keypad, and the four round buttons on the right read as decoration until you press one. The game's own caption — "Click into color Button firstly" — is the hint, and it is on screen the whole time you are failing.</li>
      <li><b>Hunting a key for the wooden door.</b> There is a key in this level and it does not go in that door. The wooden door is the colour lock; the key goes in the chest. The green Book with a key on its cover is the reason half of this confusion exists.</li>
      <li><b>Never examining the Folded paper.</b> A folded scrap in the bag looks like clutter. It is E — a quarter of the answer — and it only becomes E after you press Examine.</li>
      <li><b>Reading the framed wall as a code.</b> A6467, 430, and a lone I on a wall above a sofa is exactly the shape of a puzzle, and it is not one. Nothing in either recording enters any of it anywhere.</li>
      <li><b>Treating the back room as the exit.</b> There is no way out of it. Once the colour door is open, the back room is a treasure room: three plates, a chest, a bear, a knife.</li>
      <li><b>Missing the Metal plate because it is not paper.</b> E, X and T are paper. I is a metal tag. If you scan the inventory for "notes", you will count three and wonder where the fourth letter went.</li>
      <li><b>Looking for a fourth digit in the wrong place.</b> T=1 is inside the chest, and the chest needs the key, and the key needs the bear, and the bear needs the knife. That is a four-link chain for one digit, and it is the longest chain in the level.</li>
      <li><b>Assuming the codes rotate.</b> They do not. Both of our recordings, played independently, end on the same 01 · 03 · 06 · 04 and the same 5831. The Bookstore is a fixed-code level; if a code is refused, it is a wrong entry, not a reroll.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warn">
      <b>What this page could not verify.</b> Four things, plus one caveat about the numbers themselves, stated plainly rather than papered over. <b>(1)</b> The layout of the book-cover board: our frame reads disagree on how many covers are on it — nine in one pass, twelve or more in another — so this page describes the matching rule and not the grid. <b>(2)</b> The exact pickup that produces <b>X=8</b>: the desk drawer opens and the note appears in the bag a few seconds later, but no legible pickup label ties the two together on camera. <b>(3)</b> The <b>A6467</b> plate, the <b>430</b> plaque and the single letter <b>I</b> mounted on the wall: they are on the wall, they look like a code, and neither recording enters them anywhere — but "not used" is an observation from two runs, not a guarantee about every build. <b>(4)</b> The bottle-and-cork chain: both runs pick up a <b>Bottle</b> and a <b>Cork</b>, and neither gives a clean read of what the pair produces, so the page stops at the items and does not invent a step. One more honesty note: our independent recount of the poster's tiny bunting flags came out one flag different from the note on the purple string, so the figures printed here are the ones both locks accepted, not our own tally.
    </div>

